 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Charge pump",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Four external 0.1 µF capacitors let the on-chip pump double the 5 V supply to V+ and invert it to V-; those two rails power the line drivers. V+ and V- are meant only for their reservoir capacitors."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drivers",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Each of the two drivers turns a TTL/CMOS level into an inverted RS-232 level. DIN has no pull-up inside, so an unused or idle input must be held at a defined level, never left floating. The outputs survive a short to ground; the short-circuit duration rating is continuous."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Receivers",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The two receivers are Schmitt-trigger inputs that turn RS-232 levels into inverted 5 V TTL/CMOS levels. They accept up to ±30 V and resolve inputs as small as ±3 V. Each input is loaded by about 5 kΩ to ground inside, and an open input gives a high ROUT; the receivers are fail-safe for both open and shorted inputs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply and power-off",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "VCC is 4.5 V to 5.5 V and is taken from the same supply as the logic on DIN and ROUT. With VCC removed the part may be plugged into a live RS-232 device without harm; driver output resistance when unpowered is 300 Ω min."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitor selection",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "C1 to C4 need 0.1 µF and their type matters little; ceramic is the suggested dielectric, and values up to 10 µF do no harm. Going larger makes the V+ and V- rails stiffer (lower source impedance). Check that the capacitance does not fall too far over temperature, choosing about twice the nominal value when unsure. ESR, which tends to climb when cold, sets how much the two pump rails ripple. Bypass VCC with at least 0.1 µF; where pump noise on the supply matters, use a VCC capacitor at least as large as C1 to C4."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD protection",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "All pins carry the usual handling protection. The line pins (driver outputs and receiver inputs) add structures that withstand ±15 kV human-body-model discharges with the part unpowered. The HBM test discharges a 100 pF capacitor through 1.5 kΩ into the pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Route the pump capacitor traces short, above all at C1 and C2, whose nodes switch fastest. Give the GND pin the lowest impedance path to the board's ground plane that you can, with wide copper and several vias at both sides of the pin, for the best ESD result. The example layout places C1 across pins 1 and 3, C3 from pin 2 to ground, C2 across pins 4 and 5, C4 from pin 6 to ground, and the VCC capacitor between pins 16 and 15."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Connections",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Pins 9 to 12 go to a UART or general logic lines; pins 7, 8, 13 and 14 go to the connector or cable."
  }
 ],
